<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Roman roads</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">from c. 300 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as viae Romanae, via Romana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Roman roads were the network of routes built from about 300 BCE through the expansion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, carrying armies, officials, messages and trade overland. The main roads were often paved with stone or gravel, cambered for drainage and laid along carefully surveyed courses, with cuttings and bridges over rivers and ravines. At the empire's height 29 military highways radiated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and 372 great roads linked its 113 provinces, more than 400,000 kilometres in all, over 80,500 of them paved. Many of their courses survived for millennia, some beneath modern roads.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Roman roads</b> remained routes for centuries, and Hadrian's Wall is now a World Heritage Site.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Roman roads</b>, aqueducts, bridges and urban planning shaped the geography of European settlement for centuries after the empire's fall; many modern cities, among them London, Paris, Cologne and Vienna, grew directly from Roman castra (forts) or administrative centres.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Roman roads</b> such as the Via Militaris, the Via Egnatia, the Via de Zenta and the Kopaonik road carried wine, manufactures and luxury goods from the coast and metals, cattle, timber, wool, skins and leather from the interior.</blockquote><a href="/empire/serbian-empire">Serbian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
